Original commit body from e718dd46d75d38d4373fabe5170f1532355e45fd:

Both analyzers resolved a call by the callee's simple name across the whole
project. The import hint they passed the resolver is a DOTTED path, which it
compares against FILE paths, so it never matched, and an exact name match
returns before any hint is read. spark's `import scala.sys.process.Process`
then `Process(cmd).!` bound to a test `case class Process` in another package;
scala3's to the sbt build's `object Process`. The edge was false and the launch
never reached the catalogue.

kotlin (qualified names are reliable there): a symbol the resolver returns
stands only when its qualified name is the import or lies under it (the default
`Companion` segment dropped). Otherwise the call binds to the project symbol AT
the imported path, found in a map of every file's symbols (the resolver keeps
one per name), or is withheld. A project type keeps the placeholder module slot
when its import names a project path (WI-kilap).

scala (a symbol's name omits its enclosing object, a package-object member's
package omits the object, imports may be relative): an import CONTRADICTS the
returned symbol only when no reading of it fits: a segment-aligned suffix of
<package>.<name>; a start at some trailing run of the symbol's package; or a
leaf naming the symbol or its enclosing type, through a type the symbol's own
file declares. An import whose head the file itself imported is read through
it; `_root_.` is absolute; a block-scoped import applies only inside its block.
A contradicted call is left unresolved with the import path in its module slot.

